1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,430
Two, five, ten, seventeen.

2
00:00:02,430 --> 00:00:03,595
What comes next?

3
00:00:03,995 --> 00:00:06,042
Some friends guess twenty four.

4
00:00:06,042 --> 00:00:07,592
Some guess twenty six.

5
00:00:07,592 --> 00:00:08,644
Who is right?

6
00:00:09,094 --> 00:00:12,117
Diya found this puzzle on
a quiz card in the canteen.

7
00:00:12,467 --> 00:00:16,690
Is there a sure way to find the next
number, or the hundredth number, without

8
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:17,590
guessing?

9
00:00:17,831 --> 00:00:18,731
There is.

10
00:00:18,592 --> 00:00:19,641
Find the rule.

11
00:00:19,641 --> 00:00:21,830
Today we meet sequences and series.

12
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:25,051
Think of a queue at the canteen counter.

13
00:00:25,051 --> 00:00:27,085
Every person stands in a place.

14
00:00:27,085 --> 00:00:28,667
First, second, third.

15
00:00:29,067 --> 00:00:31,726
Each person holds a token with a number.

16
00:00:31,726 --> 00:00:33,022
The place matters.

17
00:00:33,022 --> 00:00:35,671
Swap two people, and the queue changes.

18
00:00:36,071 --> 00:00:37,884
A sequence is exactly that.

19
00:00:37,884 --> 00:00:41,358
Numbers arranged in a definite
order, following a rule.

20
00:00:42,008 --> 00:00:43,966
Each number is called a term.

21
00:00:43,966 --> 00:00:45,687
The first term is a one.

22
00:00:45,687 --> 00:00:47,455
The second term is a two.

23
00:00:47,855 --> 00:00:50,270
The term in place n is called a n.

24
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:53,073
It is the nth term, or the general term.

25
00:00:53,473 --> 00:00:56,866
Here, n is the place, like
the place in the queue.

26
00:00:56,866 --> 00:00:59,291
And a n is the number standing there.

27
00:00:59,941 --> 00:01:02,361
Now let us find the
rule for Diya's puzzle.

28
00:01:02,361 --> 00:01:03,482
Watch n count up.

29
00:01:03,882 --> 00:01:08,085
At place one, draw a square of
one dot, and add one extra dot.

30
00:01:08,085 --> 00:01:09,214
That makes two.

31
00:01:09,614 --> 00:01:13,678
At place two, a two by two
square is four dots, plus one.

32
00:01:13,678 --> 00:01:14,578
Five.

33
00:01:14,235 --> 00:01:16,437
At place three, nine plus one.

34
00:01:16,437 --> 00:01:17,337
Ten.

35
00:01:17,345 --> 00:01:20,583
So the rule is a n equals
n squared plus one.

36
00:01:20,583 --> 00:01:23,217
At place five, twenty five plus one.

37
00:01:23,217 --> 00:01:26,538
The next number is twenty
six, not twenty four.

38
00:01:27,188 --> 00:01:28,781
And the hundredth term?

39
00:01:28,781 --> 00:01:30,869
One hundred squared, plus one.

40
00:01:30,869 --> 00:01:32,365
Ten thousand and one.

41
00:01:32,365 --> 00:01:33,705
No guessing needed.

42
00:01:34,355 --> 00:01:35,276
Side by side.

43
00:01:35,276 --> 00:01:37,349
There are two ways to give a rule.

44
00:01:37,749 --> 00:01:41,233
A formula rule gives any
term straight from its place.

45
00:01:41,233 --> 00:01:43,363
a n equals n squared plus one.

46
00:01:43,763 --> 00:01:46,803
A step rule builds each term
from the ones before it.

47
00:01:46,803 --> 00:01:50,681
In the Fibonacci sequence, each term
is the sum of the two terms before

48
00:01:50,681 --> 00:01:51,581
it.

49
00:01:51,074 --> 00:01:53,474
One, one, two, three, five, eight.

50
00:01:54,074 --> 00:01:55,084
Now you try.

51
00:01:55,084 --> 00:01:58,793
If a n equals two n plus
one, what is the tenth term?

52
00:01:58,793 --> 00:02:00,000
Pause and think.

53
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:04,100
Twenty one!

54
00:02:04,093 --> 00:02:05,551
Put n equal to ten.

55
00:02:05,551 --> 00:02:07,348
Two times ten, plus one.

56
00:02:07,998 --> 00:02:10,824
Sequences can also be finite or infinite.

57
00:02:11,224 --> 00:02:15,074
A finite sequence stops, like
the ages of five friends.

58
00:02:15,074 --> 00:02:19,184
An infinite sequence never
stops, like all the even numbers.

59
00:02:19,784 --> 00:02:20,850
Now, a series.

60
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:24,464
Take the terms of a sequence,
and put plus signs between them.

61
00:02:24,864 --> 00:02:27,632
One, three, five, seven is a sequence.

62
00:02:27,632 --> 00:02:30,765
One plus three plus five
plus seven is a series.

63
00:02:31,165 --> 00:02:33,267
Watch the odd numbers add up.

64
00:02:33,267 --> 00:02:36,686
Each one wraps around a
square, like a letter L.

65
00:02:36,686 --> 00:02:39,257
The total is always a perfect square.

66
00:02:39,857 --> 00:02:43,781
We write a series in short
with sigma, the Greek letter S.

67
00:02:43,781 --> 00:02:47,923
It means: add up the terms a
k, from k equals one, to n.

68
00:02:48,673 --> 00:02:49,573
Quick one.

69
00:02:49,467 --> 00:02:52,535
Is two plus four plus six
a sequence or a series?

70
00:02:52,535 --> 00:02:53,658
Pause and think.

71
00:02:56,858 --> 00:02:57,758
A series!

72
00:02:57,543 --> 00:02:59,442
The plus signs make it a series.

73
00:02:59,442 --> 00:03:02,101
Without them, two,
four, six is a sequence.

74
00:03:02,751 --> 00:03:03,765
Remember this.

75
00:03:03,765 --> 00:03:05,820
A sequence is a list in order.

76
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:07,676
A series adds that list up.

77
00:03:08,376 --> 00:03:10,068
Now, one worked example.

78
00:03:10,068 --> 00:03:12,402
This one is from the NCERT exercise.

79
00:03:12,752 --> 00:03:17,544
Write the first five terms of the
sequence a n equals n times n plus two.

80
00:03:18,044 --> 00:03:18,944
Step one.

81
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:20,268
Put n equal to one.

82
00:03:20,268 --> 00:03:21,993
One times three is three.

83
00:03:22,743 --> 00:03:23,643
Step two.

84
00:03:23,538 --> 00:03:25,437
n equal to two gives eight.

85
00:03:25,437 --> 00:03:27,530
n equal to three gives fifteen.

86
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:29,180
Step three.

87
00:03:29,148 --> 00:03:31,384
n equal to four gives twenty four.

88
00:03:31,384 --> 00:03:33,620
n equal to five gives thirty five.

89
00:03:34,370 --> 00:03:38,635
So the sequence starts three, eight,
fifteen, twenty four, thirty five.

90
00:03:38,635 --> 00:03:41,890
Add them, and the series of
five terms is eighty five.

91
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:44,001
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:03:44,351 --> 00:03:48,681
For a n equals n squared plus one,
many students say the first term is

93
00:03:48,681 --> 00:03:49,581
one.

94
00:03:49,167 --> 00:03:51,565
They start counting at n equals zero.

95
00:03:51,965 --> 00:03:55,029
But places start at
one, just like a queue.

96
00:03:55,029 --> 00:03:58,929
So the first term is one
squared plus one, which is two.

97
00:03:59,579 --> 00:04:00,878
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:00,878 --> 00:04:05,117
In JEE Main, as a sequence given by
a step rule, where you find a pattern

99
00:04:05,117 --> 00:04:06,286
for the nth term.

100
00:04:06,886 --> 00:04:08,014
Exam level now.

101
00:04:08,014 --> 00:04:10,424
Find the tenth term of this sequence.

102
00:04:10,424 --> 00:04:11,600
Pause and solve.

103
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,318
The answer is A.

104
00:04:18,318 --> 00:04:20,363
One thousand and twenty five.

105
00:04:20,863 --> 00:04:22,560
Write the first few terms.

106
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:24,614
Three, five, nine, seventeen.

107
00:04:25,114 --> 00:04:27,971
Each one is a power of two, plus one.

108
00:04:27,971 --> 00:04:32,529
Two plus one, four plus one,
eight plus one, sixteen plus one.

109
00:04:32,529 --> 00:04:35,240
So a n is two to the n, plus one.

110
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:37,617
Check it with the step rule.

111
00:04:37,617 --> 00:04:42,344
Double it and take away one, and you
get the next power of two, plus one.

112
00:04:42,344 --> 00:04:43,244
It works.

113
00:04:43,109 --> 00:04:46,911
So the tenth term is one thousand
and twenty four, plus one.

114
00:04:47,511 --> 00:04:49,051
Here is your recap.

115
00:04:49,051 --> 00:04:50,634
Screenshot this card.

116
00:04:53,834 --> 00:04:56,020
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

117
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:57,320
One.

118
00:04:56,837 --> 00:04:59,785
What is the difference between
a sequence and a series?

119
00:05:00,685 --> 00:05:01,585
Two.

120
00:05:01,144 --> 00:05:04,351
Find the fifth term of a n
equals three n minus one.

121
00:05:05,251 --> 00:05:06,151
Three.

122
00:05:05,812 --> 00:05:09,519
Write the first four terms of
a n equals n squared plus one.

123
00:05:10,619 --> 00:05:13,786
Next time: Aarav saves the
same amount every week.

124
00:05:13,786 --> 00:05:17,561
That makes a very special sequence,
the arithmetic progression.
